{
  "action": "read_weather",
  "note": "Eight seconds. The tin is sealed, the drawer is locked, and only wire weather is cleared for air. Last time I was in that booth I pulled the loudspeaker plug myself before the archive head could pass — I made sure the public meter sat at zero. Rita re-locked the drawer. That was the shape of the decision: the clip stays in the tin, the air gets the wire. I'm not going to walk up to Rita with eight seconds on the red lamp and ask her to break her own seal, especially not for a slot that's already been assigned to the printed line. Pittsburgh is waiting on a weather read, not on a negotiation. I take the line, I read the wire numbers, I get the routine sign-off, and my name stays clean. The tin stays in the drawer. It was sealed when I put it back, it's sealed now, and it's sealed when I walk out. That's the job. The wire is the weather. I read it."
}
